# glow_desk_run_witness.rish -- every runnable Glow desk actually run, derived from the room.
#
# glow/gen/ holds the generated desk corpus for the Glow language. Until 20260907 exactly one guard
# ran any of it -- tools/g/glow_run_desk_witness.rish, 1,133 lines naming 218 desks one block at a
# time -- and the row booked at 20260907.110022 named what that shape costs: a hand-written enumeration standing in for a
# population reads as coverage from every side, and it drifts the moment the population grows.
#
# This guard runs tools/fixtures/g/glow_desk_run_scan.sh, which DERIVES its population from the room
# on every pass. The selection is every desk under glow/gen/ minus those declaring they must not run
# (by either marker), plus every sample-taking desk that declares its own sample in its head band --
# 347 desks measured 20260908, the whole runnable room, where the elder witness names 218.
#
# THE COST, AND WHY THE BATCH IS CHEAPER THAN THE BLOCKS. tools/g/glow_run_worker.sh rebuilds
# glow/bin/glow_run from glow/glow_run.rye on every invocation, which is right for a hand running one
# desk and wasteful 347 times over. Measured on this pier 20260907: 3.80s per desk through the
# worker, of which 2.42s is that rebuild -- so the elder witness spends roughly 527s re-linking one
# unchanged binary across its 218 blocks. The batch builds the compiler once.
#
# THE 46 SAMPLE-TAKING DESKS JOINED THE SELECTION ON 20260908. A desk the worker will only run with
# an argument was excluded because choosing that argument was a judgment per desk rather than a
# loop, and the only place named to keep 46 answers was a fourth hand-written enumeration -- the
# shape REDS %532 booked. Each desk carries its own instead: `::  Sample: 3 5` in the same six-line
# head band the run-contract is read from. The scan lowers those with --sample-argv and hands the
# words to the built binary; a runnable sampled desk declaring nothing is refused by name. All 46
# values were run on metal before they were written, 46 of 46 GREEN through tools/g/glow_run.rish.
#
# THE CHEAP LEGS RUN FIRST, and that ordering is load-bearing rather than tidy. The two stub legs
# below take seconds; the live pass takes minutes of Zig builds. Running the live pass first means a
# fault in a stub leg is found after the expensive work rather than before it, which is exactly how
# this file's own first version was written -- and it cost a full batch to learn.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/g/glow_desk_run_witness.rish

# invariant: the refusal is reachable ON THIS SELECTION. A ceiling proven only in the passing
# direction cannot be told from a bypass. Re-running the real batch under a lower ceiling would
# prove it and cost a second pass of Zig builds for a reading the live leg already takes, so the
# proof swaps the PER-DESK RUNNER instead: a runner that always refuses turns the live 347 into 347
# failures and the ceiling must bite, and a runner that always passes must read zero and ok. Both
# legs walk the same derived selection the live leg walks, so what they prove is this room's reading
# rather than a pen's. The swap is a proof seam and never a bypass -- the live leg below runs with
# it unset, and that is the only reading which speaks for the corpus.
#
# The stubs are `false` and `true` as bare words rather than /bin/false and /bin/true, and the
# difference is not cosmetic. This pier's /bin holds exactly one entry, sh; a stub named by absolute
# path fails to EXECUTE, which reads as a refusal -- so the refusing leg passed while proving
# nothing, and only the passing leg could tell. A welcome asserted as hard as a refusal is what
# caught it.
let all_fail = run ["sh" "-c" "GLOW_DESK_RUN_ONE=false sh tools/fixtures/g/glow_desk_run_scan.sh"]
assert (all_fail.ok == false) else "glow_desk_run: a runner that refuses every desk must break the ceiling"
assert all_fail.out contains "verdict=over_failed_ceiling" else "glow_desk_run: want the ceiling refusal named -- ${all_fail.out}"
assert all_fail.out contains "failed=347" else "glow_desk_run: every selected desk must reach the runner -- ${all_fail.out}"

let all_pass = run ["sh" "-c" "GLOW_DESK_RUN_ONE=true sh tools/fixtures/g/glow_desk_run_scan.sh"]
assert all_pass.ok else "glow_desk_run: a runner that passes every desk must read ok: ${all_pass.out}"
assert all_pass.out contains "failed=0" else "glow_desk_run: want zero failures from a passing runner -- ${all_pass.out}"

# invariant: the reading is proven on real desk rooms rather than trusted. The control builds
# miniature desk rooms in a throwaway pen and plants each refusal, then lifts it -- 95 behaviors,
# both sides of both ceilings, both declaration markers separately, the head band's own edge read
# from both the run-contract and the sample side, a declared sample proven to reach the runner as
# split words, and --list proven to hand the runner nothing. The per-desk run there is a stub on purpose: the control
# proves WHICH desks are selected and how they are counted, and the live leg below proves that they
# lower, build and run on metal.
let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/g/glow_desk_run_control.sh"]
assert control.ok else "glow_desk_run control failed: ${control.out}"
assert control.out contains "fail=0" else "glow_desk_run control reported failures: ${control.out}"

# THE LIVE PASS, last because it is the expensive one.
let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/g/glow_desk_run_scan.sh"]
assert scan.ok else "glow_desk_run scan failed: ${scan.err}${scan.out}"

# invariant: the corpus is present -- a scan reading zero desks has lost its subject, and every
# reading below would then be trivially satisfied by an empty room.
assert scan.out contains "desks=" else "glow_desk_run scan printed no desk count"

# invariant: every selected desk was handed to the runner. `ran` and `selected` are counted in two
# different places -- one off the file list, one inside the loop -- so their agreement is a reading
# rather than a restatement.
assert scan.out contains "selected=347" else "glow_desk_run: the runnable selection stands at 347 -- a desk landed, left, or changed its declaration"
assert scan.out contains "ran=347" else "glow_desk_run: the loop must run every desk the selection names -- ${scan.out}"

# invariant: the failure count only falls. It stands at 3 rather than 0 because three files under
# glow/gen/ are data fixtures rather than desks and carry no marker in either name or head --
# sample-demo-fact-line-lits, sample-demo-fixture-lits and sample-digraph-table. Giving them one
# changes what declares a desk's kind, which is a language custody ruling rather than a repair a lap
# takes (REDS %532).
#
# THIS COMMENT READ "the three refuse in two distinct ways: two at TooManyLines and one at
# unsupported Glow head" until 20260908.234354, and metal answers THREE ways across BOTH of
# glow_run's exit codes. That elder line was itself a correction to `%532`, which had recorded one
# refusal for all three -- so a correction that caught one-for-three made the same shape of error at
# two-for-three, and its grouping crossed the very line glow_run draws:
#
#   sample-digraph-table.glow        exit 2  unsupported Glow head    glow_run DECLINES the file
#   sample-demo-fixture-lits.glow    exit 2  too many Glow lines      glow_run DECLINES the file
#   sample-demo-fact-line-lits.glow  exit 1  multi lower failed       taken as a desk, then broke
#
# The two called one refusal sit on opposite sides of declined-versus-broke, and the one set apart
# shares a side with one of them. The elder grouping was made by matching the word TooManyLines,
# which is the one reading glow_desk_run_scan.sh's own door says not to make.
#
# So the split is asserted from the scan rather than recited from a sentence: the parts below are
# derived from glow_run's exit status on every pass, and a fourth fixture landing tomorrow moves
# the reading that names its own fault.
assert scan.out contains "failed_ceiling=3" else "glow_desk_run: the ceiling stands where it was measured -- a ceiling only falls"
assert scan.out contains "failed_declined=2" else "glow_desk_run: two of the three fixtures are DECLINED by glow_run (exit 2) -- ${scan.out}"
assert scan.out contains "failed_lower=1" else "glow_desk_run: one of the three is taken as a desk and breaks in the lowerer (exit 1) -- ${scan.out}"
assert scan.out contains "failed_build=0" else "glow_desk_run: no selected desk fails to build -- ${scan.out}"
assert scan.out contains "failed_run=0" else "glow_desk_run: no built desk exits nonzero -- ${scan.out}"
assert scan.out contains "failed_unreadable=0" else "glow_desk_run: no selected desk is unreadable -- glow_run exit 3, seated 20260911.230925 -- ${scan.out}"
assert scan.out contains "failed_unclassified=0" else "glow_desk_run: every failure is named by its stage -- ${scan.out}"
assert scan.out contains "verdict=ok" else "glow_desk_run: the scan refused -- ${scan.out}"

say "GREEN: glow_desk_run -- 347 runnable desks derived from the room and run, 301 bare and 46 on the sample each declares for itself, 3 unmarked data fixtures at their ceiling split by stage (2 declined, 1 broken in the lowerer), both runner-swap legs proven, 109 control behaviors green."
